<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Mazepa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a>–<a href="/years/1709">1709</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Mazepa was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> from 1687 to 1708 and a generous patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches, learning and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> he broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> in 1708, hoping to free the Hetmanate from Russian control. After their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709 he fled to Ottoman territory, where he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa.jpg" alt="Ivan Mazepa" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Bernigeroth (de) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iwan_Mazepa_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
